gap_analysis.detail:
FileWriter (lines ~128, 140-145, 414-416) and PublishWriter (lines ~65, 71, 592-594) both re-implement the same business rule: 'only the checked-out user (or full-control admin) may operate on a checked-out file.' This guard belongs on the FileVersionManager or a File domain method. Currently no domain object knows it is checked out — callers inspect raw IsCheckedOut + CheckedOutUserId fields on a data bag.

Before (PublishWriter.cs):
  if (file.IsCheckedOut && file.CheckedOutUserId != context.UserId)
    throw new NoPermissionException(teamSiteId, context.UserId, request.FileId);

After (ItemModel.cs — domain object):
  public void EnsureOperableBy(string userId, bool isFullControlUser) {
    if (IsCheckedOut && CheckedOutUserId != userId && !isFullControlUser)
      throw new NoPermissionException(TeamSiteId, userId, Id);
  }

ContentViewWriter.AddContentViewDefinition contains pin-count enforcement (>= CONTENT_VIEW_PINNED_NUMBER_LIMIT), total-view-count enforcement (>= CONTENT_VIEW_NUMBER_LIMIT), name-uniqueness checks, and standard-view-name deduplication. None of these are on a ContentView domain object. The domain layer has no ContentView aggregate — ContentView is a POJO written and read entirely from the Express layer with raw Dapper.

Before (ContentViewWriter.cs):
  if (contentViewCustomizedDatas.Count(cv => cv.Pinned) >= CMConstant.CONTENT_VIEW_PINNED_NUMBER_LIMIT)
    throw new ContentViewsPinnedLimitException(...);

After (ContentViewDefinitionModel.cs):
  public static ContentViewDefinitionModel Create(IEnumerable<ContentViewDefinitionModel> existing, ContentViewRequest request) {
    if (existing.Count(v => v.Pinned) >= MAX_PINNED) throw new ContentViewsPinnedLimitException(...);
    if (existing.Count >= MAX_VIEWS) throw new ContentViewsLimitException(...);
    return new ContentViewDefinitionModel(request);
  }

At least 7 files in Seismic.ContentManager.Express use Dapper directly (FileReader, TeamSiteReader, ContentViewReader, FlexibleContentQueryReader, PermissionReader, ActivationWriter, ContentViewWriter, SBPProfileWriter, ExternalSyncWriter). The Express project is the application orchestration layer; it should call domain repositories, not execute SQL. This means business queries bypass the domain model entirely and cannot be tested without a live database.

Before (FileReader.cs):
  using Dapper;
  var results = connection.Query<ItemModel>(sql, parameters);

After: route through IFileRepository.QueryFiles(criteria) implemented in FileRepository (domain infra layer).

FolderRepository.GetFolder and GetFolderOriginItemId construct SqlParameter arrays and call ExecuteSqlReader directly — the domain class is aware of SqlConnection, SqlDbType, and table schema. The domain project should depend on an IFolderRepository interface backed by an infrastructure adapter, never on System.Data.SqlClient.

Before (FolderRepository.cs):
  SqlParameter[] parameters = { new SqlParameter("@id", SqlDbType.NVarChar) { Value = folderId } };
  using (var reader = ExecuteSqlReader(sql, parameters)) { ... }

After: Extract to Seismic.ContentManagerService.LibraryService.Infrastructure/Folder/FolderSqlRepository.cs implementing IFolderRepository.

ContentObjectType.IsNonPublishableType(file.TypeId.ToString()) is called in both PublishWriter and FileWriter to gate publish. This is a domain concept (a type attribute of the file) that should be a property or method on ItemModel, not a static utility called from two different application-layer classes.

Before (PublishWriter.cs, FileWriter.cs):
  if (ContentObjectType.IsNonPublishableType(file.TypeId.ToString())) throw new NoPermissionException(...);

After (ItemModel.cs):
  public bool IsPublishable => !ContentObjectType.IsNonPublishableType(TypeId.ToString());
